concepts.body:
A **variable** is a name that holds a value, like a labelled drawer. Bash variables usually hold text: a file name, a folder, a word. Create one by writing the name, `=`, and the value, with **no spaces around `=`**: `name=Ada`. With spaces, `name = Ada` would run a command called `name`.

To use the value, put `$` in front of the name: `echo $name` prints `Ada`. Bash replacing `$name` with its value is called **expansion**. Write `${name}` when other letters follow straight after, as in `${name}_backup`; otherwise bash would look for a variable called `name_backup`.

Bash stores every value as text, which programmers call a **string**. `count=5` stores the character `5` rather than the number five, and arithmetic only happens inside `$(( ))`: `$(( count + 1 ))` gives `6`. Values that contain spaces need quotes when you set them: `title="My report"`.

Using a variable that was never set silently gives empty text, so a typo in a name quietly produces nothing (a later lesson shows how to catch this). `unset name` removes a variable, and `readonly NAME=value` creates one that can't be changed.

Bash also fills in some **special variables** for you:

- `$?`: the result number of the last command, where 0 means it worked (more in the next lessons);
- `$$`: the ID number the system gave this running shell;
- `$0`, `$1`, `$2`...: a script's name (a script is a saved file of commands, covered soon) and the arguments it was given; `$#`: how many there are;
- `$RANDOM`: a random whole number; `$PWD`: the folder you're in; `$HOME`: your home folder.

A naming habit: lower-case names for your own variables, and UPPER_CASE for **environment variables**, the settings every program you start receives, such as `PATH` and `HOME`. Then you never overwrite one of those by accident.

concepts.code:
name="Ada"                  # no spaces around =
greeting="Hello, $name"
echo "$greeting"            # Hello, Ada
echo "${name}_backup"       # Ada_backup  (braces mark where the name ends)
count=5
echo "$count + 1"           # 5 + 1       (just text)
echo "$(( count + 1 ))"     # 6           (arithmetic)
unset name
echo "[$name]"              # []          (an unset variable gives nothing)
readonly PI=3.14
declare -p PI               # declare -r PI="3.14"   (-r: read-only)
echo "shell ID $$, last result $?"

concepts.body:
A **pipe**, written `|`, connects the standard output of one command to the standard input of the next. A **pipeline** like `a | b | c` chains several. All the commands run at the same time and the text flows through them piece by piece, so pipelines can handle files far bigger than the computer could hold at once, and the first results appear quickly.

The usual style is to build pipelines from small tools that each do one job:

- `grep` keeps the lines that contain some text;
- `sort` sorts lines, and `uniq -c` counts repeated lines that sit next to each other;
- `head` and `tail` keep the first or last lines, and `wc -l` counts lines;
- `cut`, `tr`, `awk` and `sed` pick out or change parts of each line.

A typical recipe for a top-10 list is `sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head`.

Only standard output goes into the pipe. To send error messages along too, use `2>&1 |` or bash's shorthand `|&`.

A pipeline's exit status is the status of its **last** command, so `failing-command | tee log.txt` looks successful (`tee` copies its input both to the screen and into a file). `set -o pipefail` makes the whole pipeline fail if any part fails, and you'll usually want it on. Bash also keeps each part's status in `PIPESTATUS`.

When a later command stops reading early (say `head -n 1` exits after one line), the command feeding it is told nobody is listening any more, and stops quietly. That's normal, and it's why `yes | head -n 3` finishes even though `yes` would repeat forever.

concepts.code:
usage() { echo "usage: tool [-v] [-o FILE] NAME..." >&2; return 2; }
tool() {
  local verbose=0 output=- opt OPTIND=1
  while getopts ':vo:h' opt; do
    case $opt in
      v) verbose=1 ;;
      o) output=$OPTARG ;;
      h) usage; return ;;
      :) echo "-$OPTARG needs a value" >&2; usage; return ;;
      \?) echo "unknown option -$OPTARG" >&2; usage; return ;;
    esac
  done
  shift $((OPTIND - 1))        # "$@" is now just the operands
  (( $# > 0 )) || { usage; return; }
  echo "verbose=$verbose output=$output names=$*"
}
tool -v -o out.txt a b    # verbose=1 output=out.txt names=a b
tool -vo out.txt a        # verbose=1 output=out.txt names=a  (bundled)
tool -x a                 # unknown option -x / usage: ...
tool -o                   # -o needs a value / usage: ...
